🚀 Amazon EFS with EKS (EFS CSI Driver)

Amazon EFS (Elastic File System) is a regional, fully managed NFS filesystem.
When used in EKS with the EFS CSI driver, it provides shared, cross-AZ persistent storage for Kubernetes workloads.

✅ Why EFS is useful in EKS?
1. Multi-AZ Storage

Unlike EBS (AZ-bound), EFS works across all AZs.

So your Pods can run in:

AZ-a

AZ-b

AZ-c

All can mount the same filesystem at the same time.

2. Shared File Storage

Multiple Pods across nodes and AZs can read/write the same directory.

Perfect for:

Web servers needing shared /uploads

ML pipelines

CI agents

Shared config/data dirs

3. Automatic Scaling

EFS grows/shrinks automatically.
No need to pick disk size.

You pay only for actual usage.

4. Managed NFS Server

You don’t manage:

servers

capacity

replication

failover

AWS handles it.

🧩 How EFS works inside EKS (with CSI Driver)

Deploy EFS CSI Driver
(Usually via eks-addons or Helm)

Create an EFS File System
(Regional, multi-AZ)

Attach mount targets in each AZ where your nodes exist

Create:

StorageClass

PersistentVolume (static) or

AccessPoint (preferred)

Create PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)

Pods mount PVC and access the filesystem via NFS
